# TODO - Malbolge layout and encoding backend

## Objective

Implement address-sensitive instruction layout, self-modification planning,
encoding, jumps, data placement, runtime linkage, and final `.malbolge`
emission.

## Acceptance

- Layout solves address-sensitive decode and self-modification constraints
  reproducibly and emits only verifier-accepted `.malbolge` for the declared
  profile.
- The backend may additionally emit an annotated human/debug view, but
  canonicalizing that view must reproduce the executable `.malbolge` stream
  byte-for-byte; comments and layout never occupy target positions.
- The stage has deterministic input/output form, rejects malformed or
  unsupported input explicitly, and preserves source/profile provenance needed
  downstream.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder implementation or
  undocumented workaround required for this objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- A first address-sensitive executable machine-code slice now exists under
  `src/compiler/malbolge-layout/`. It assigns exact code addresses, inverts
  normative graphical decoding over all 94 source-cell phases, preserves the
  immutable profile ID/fingerprint, and independently admits each emitted
  byte. Accepted operations are no-op, machine I/O, rotate/crazy, a single
  optional data-pointer relocation (`j`) before arithmetic, and final halt.
  Two to 4096 code cells are admitted; early/missing halts reject.
- Crucial alias evidence: data and code pointers normally advance together,
  so an unplaced rotate/crazy can modify its own code cell and trigger a VM
  encryption error. Relocated arithmetic requires the original encoded `j`
  source-memory word **at the current data pointer** to address data beyond
  the complete emitted source length. The single `j` may follow a no-op,
  byte-I/O or strictly forward-jump prefix; a jump can alter `D` relative to
  `C` and XLAT2-encrypt the eventual relocation operand. Later code must be
  jump-free and arithmetic may operate only after the proven relocation.
  A later-phase VM regression admits a larger safe code/data layout than
  phase zero, proves code-pointer/encryption state stepwise, and rejects a
  one-cell extension. Every safely reachable rotate/crazy phase from 1 to 93
  now has separate profile-derived decoder and witness-size regression coverage.
  Missing, duplicate, early-arithmetic, post-relocation jumps or overlapping
  relocations fail closed.
- `tests/malbolge_layout.rs` checks all 94 decode phases for both canonical
  profile byte-I/O operations, executes actual byte I/O and relocated ternary
  rotate/crazy instructions through the current and historical profile VMs,
  and rejects source/profile tampering, invalid lengths, early halt and unsafe
  data placements. First forward `i` jump and a strictly increasing chain
  now prove each code destination from the *evolving data-pointer* memory
  word, including the operand overwritten by an earlier XLAT2 encryption.
  The verifier tracks the bounded source-memory shadow, checks all skipped
  positions are inert no-op padding, and rejects forged/retrograde targets,
  effectful skipped gaps and mixing jumps with data relocation. Real current
  and historical VMs execute 2- and 3-jump programs including operand reads
  encrypted during a prior skip **or** normal code execution, with byte I/O
  through the jumps. The independently admitted
  layout now returns exact ordered code, data and encryption positions plus
  optional planned rotate/crazy data-write addresses for every reached step.
  The data-write witness requires addresses outside all source cells; real
  normative VM traces check those semantic data reads and changed-cell deltas
  (including unchanged-value writes), and post-step XLAT2 code-cell values.
  New real VM cases admit a jump followed by relocated rotate/crazy when
  the *actual* data-pointer operand proves code/data separation, including
  one operand previously encrypted by the first jump. Extending source
  beyond that exact dynamic bound rejects; later jumps remain forbidden.
  The original full 94-phase single-jump decoder matrix remains covered.
  General source-to-target lowering, backward/mutable code jumps, heap data,
  guest runtime linkage and self-modifying revisits remain unimplemented.
- A bounded library-level auto-padding planner now accepts symbolic
  forward-jump steps, derives each target from the live data-pointer word and
  its exact XLAT2 shadow, inserts inert graphical no-ops, and returns a
  logical-to-physical source map plus independently admitted linear layout.
  A per-physical-cell origin witness now distinguishes requested instructions,
  executed relocation padding and skipped forward-jump padding; the evidence
  is built from the independently admitted reached code path, not from
  inferred source-C positions.
  All 94 initial jump decoder phases, 1-to-3-hop paths and jump-then-`j`
  relocation have focused real-VM evidence for both canonical profiles;
  invalid short/backward edges and oversized layouts fail closed. The
  default machine-token CLI still takes concrete jump addresses, while an
  opt-in `--auto-layout` mode now accepts symbolic jumps. Neither mode
  implements conditional branches or loops.
- The same library planner now accepts `AutoRelocate`, searching at most 94
  reached no-op decoder phases for a safe `j` operand and preserving the live
  data pointer, encrypted operands, source length and planned data writes.
  Focused normative VM tests pass long-suffix and two-jump relocation with
  rotate/crazy, while duplicate/unsafe relocation and subsequent jump reject.
  The default CLI remains concrete-token only; opt-in `--auto-layout`
  admits symbolic `auto-relocate` but not general control-flow lowering.
- The file publisher now exposes the proven planner under explicit
  `--auto-layout` syntax, accepting symbolic `jump` and `auto-relocate`
  alongside bounded concrete machine effects. The expanded source is
  independently admitted against exact profile identity, checked by the
  normative VM for its *reached* step count, then published through the
  existing no-clobber raw/capsule path. Process-level current/historical
  profile tests prove byte-identical concrete/auto layout, VM I/O and
  relocated arithmetic, including two forward jumps and encrypted data
  operands. The separate real `malbolge --input-stdin` runner also replays
  generated historical raw and modern capsule output for successful input
  bytes `0x00`/`0xff`, without implying EOF or full guest ABI support.
  A second three-hop process fixture proves byte-identical source versus
  the manual concrete backend even when the third jump consumes an
  XLAT2-encrypted data operand, and confirms actual general-runner binary
  input/output in both historical raw and modern capsule modes.
  Malformed grammar, numeric jump tokens in symbolic mode and unsafe plans
  fail without a final file. Conditional branches, guest heap,
  runtime linkage and general C compilation remain open.
- A first real explicit-profile source publisher, `malbolge_layout`, now
  converts a bounded closed machine-op token list into **new** graphical
  `.malbolge` files. It re-admits source and verifies exact-step termination
  with the normative selected-profile VM before same-directory atomic,
  no-clobber publication. Process tests prove byte-exact reproducibility,
  historical/current VM I/O, real jumped and relocated machine execution,
  rejected malformed inputs, protected existing targets/symlinks and competing
  writers. Real jump-then-relocate data programs now publish raw/capsule
  files for both profiles and prove live data-word mutation with no code
  alias; an XLAT2-relocation capacity boundary rejects before publication.
  Real 3-jump programs now publish raw/capsule files under both
  profiles; independent VM reload confirms output and rejects a forged third
  target before publication. Optional `--capsule` uses the version-one builder,
  round-trips canonical ID/fingerprint and exact raw payload via its independent
  parser before publication, and executes modern output through the actual
  general `malbolge` runner. Historical interpreter tests check the frozen
  safe fallback. Unframed raw mode still needs explicit modern profile input;
  neither mode claims typed C→Malbolge lowering or guest-runtime linkage.
- The emitter now accepts a nonempty bounded canonical MCTR wire file
  through `--ternary-file`, restores it with the authoritative pre-layout
  codec, and admits only the separately validated successful-byte-echo SSA
  subset before using the *same* exact-profile VM and raw/capsule file
  publisher. A real external-process integration fixture confirms a
  three-byte trace including `0x00` and `0xff`, while profile mismatch,
  modified wire, an independently canonical but unsupported SSA trace and
  oversized files fail without a published artifact. The publisher now
  replays two successful-byte patterns in the real VM before publication,
  requiring exact output, input consumption and termination; empty-input
  preflight separately proves reachability only. Missing/empty input files,
  extra operands and existing targets reject. Neither preflight realizes
  EOF, failed I/O, guest ABI or arbitrary C execution.
- The separate opt-in `--latest-byte-trace --ternary-file` mode now publishes
  a broader, still bounded canonical MCTR successful-read trace: repeated
  outputs of the most recently read byte and consumed-but-unused reads are
  supported. Valid SSA use of an earlier read after a newer input rejects.
  Both modes share the normative VM two-pattern output/consumption/step
  preflight and atomic raw/capsule publication, and adjacent echoes yield
  identical artifacts with or without the opt-in. Real CLI process tests
  independently reopen published payloads, replay both 0 and 255 byte
  values, and confirm stale SSA, bad arguments, profile drift and corrupt
  wire fail without a final file. No EOF/error semantics or general C
  runtime have been realized.
- A separate direct canonical `--typed-file` MCTI input now admits the
  narrow zero-argument void typed successful-byte SSA echo subset without a
  caller-managed MCTR file. `--latest-byte-trace --typed-file` opts into
  repeated latest-byte output and consumed-but-unused reads, with no older
  SSA replay. The existing typed adapter revalidates canonical MCTI and
  emits MCTR and layout; the file publisher *independently* re-decodes the
  actual canonical MCTR bytes and re-encodes graphical source, comparing
  exact profile identity, bytes and VM step witness before its original
  two-pattern preflight and raw/capsule no-overwrite publish. Real process
  tests compare direct vs MCTR-file artifact bytes in both modes, verify
  guest output and consumption, and refuse wrong-profile/invalid/stale SSA,
  oversized MCTI or occupied outputs. No generic C, EOF, ABI or heap work
  is claimed.
